@Steve L

Twelve hours later, I'll add that "ta" is not a word like "lift" for "elevator" or "lorry" for "truck" that most Americans recognize as a Britishism.  It's something that is mostly unknown over here, eliciting looks of befuddlement when we come across it.

Of course, someone who watches Bridgerton or Doctor Who will probably tell me I'm way off course, but I'm talking about most people, not Anglophiles.

@Steve L 
It also helps if you know that in Greek, "polis"(=city) is feminine. Hence "nea-politan," not "neo-politan." But's that a lot to ask.
(There's some linguistic reason it's "acropolis," and not  "acrapolis," but I forget what it is. )

@Strudel Dad

