NEAPOLITAN almost got me, but I caught it and corrected NEo to NEA just in time.

@Michael

But that's not true. You dollars are "dolary" in Polish, and there is nothing wrong with it - that's how words behave in Polish. Why would it be different with Thai currency in English? I'm sure some people say BAHT in the plural when they're speaking English, but I am also sure some people say BAHTS, and they are not wrong, grammatically.

No offense was caused, obviously!

@Michael

As CB replied below, dictionaries list both baht and bahts as correct plural forms of baht.

If you insist baht is the only acceptable version, I suppose you use the singular pieróg and plural pierogi when referring to the signature Polish dish when you buy it in America, and kiełbasa and kiełbasy, too.

@CB
